  "name": "Bush band (Australian colonial folk-song revival)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Australian folk-revival band style that took up colonial bush songs, convict ballads and dance tunes collected by John Meredith and others. The Sydney group the Bushwhackers, formed in the early 1950s, set the line-up of button accordion, lagerphone (a stick nailed with bottle tops), bush bass and voices, and from the 1970s the Bushwackers of Melbourne and many bush bands played the bush dance circuit with fiddle, banjo and guitar added. The accordion card stands for the button accordion, the tambourine card stands in for the lagerphone and the tea-chest bass card for the bush bass.",
  "recipe": "classic 1950-1953 bush band, ballad Poetry, anglo singer song branch. group vocals, unprocessed natural acoustic. melodic straight accordion, tambourine orch, tea chest bass, melodic bowed song accompaniment fiddle. live tracking. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
